### What is the Observer pattern for event notification?

The Observer pattern defines a one-to-many relationship where a subject keeps a list of observers and notifies all of them when its state changes. The subject depends only on the observer interface, so adding a new reaction is adding a new observer, never editing the subject.

```java
package com.bank.observer;

import java.math.BigDecimal;
import java.util.ArrayList;
import java.util.List;
import java.util.function.Consumer;

public class StockPricePublisher {
    private final String symbol;
    private BigDecimal price;
    private final List<Consumer<PriceAlert>> observers = new ArrayList<>();

    public StockPricePublisher(String symbol, BigDecimal initialPrice) {
        this.symbol = symbol;
        this.price = initialPrice;
    }

    public void subscribe(Consumer<PriceAlert> observer) {
        observers.add(observer);
    }

    public void unsubscribe(Consumer<PriceAlert> observer) {
        observers.remove(observer);
    }

    public void setPrice(BigDecimal newPrice) {
        this.price = newPrice;
        PriceAlert alert = new PriceAlert(symbol, newPrice);
        observers.forEach(o -> o.accept(alert));
    }
}

public record PriceAlert(String symbol, BigDecimal price) {}
```

### How do you implement concrete observers?

Each observer implements the `Consumer<PriceAlert>` interface and reacts independently.

```java
package com.bank.observer;

import java.util.function.Consumer;

public class SmsAlertObserver implements Consumer<PriceAlert> {
    private final String phone;

    public SmsAlertObserver(String phone) { this.phone = phone; }

    @Override
    public void accept(PriceAlert alert) {
        System.out.println("SMS to " + phone + ": "
            + alert.symbol() + " is now INR " + alert.price());
    }
}

public class DashboardObserver implements Consumer<PriceAlert> {
    @Override
    public void accept(PriceAlert alert) {
        System.out.println("Dashboard updated: "
            + alert.symbol() + " = INR " + alert.price());
    }
}
```

```java
package com.bank.demo;

import com.bank.observer.*;
import java.math.BigDecimal;

public class ObserverDemo {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        StockPricePublisher reliance = new StockPricePublisher(
            "RELIANCE", new BigDecimal("2400"));

        reliance.subscribe(new SmsAlertObserver("+91-98765-43210"));
        reliance.subscribe(new DashboardObserver());
        reliance.subscribe(alert -> System.out.println(
            "AUDIT: Price change for " + alert.symbol()));

        reliance.setPrice(new BigDecimal("2550"));
    }
}
```

```text
SMS to +91-98765-43210: RELIANCE is now INR 2550
Dashboard updated: RELIANCE = INR 2550
AUDIT: Price change for RELIANCE
```

### How do you use Observer for order status changes?

When an order's status changes, the order fires one notification and independent observers handle different reactions.

```java
package com.bank.observer;

import java.math.BigDecimal;
import java.util.ArrayList;
import java.util.List;
import java.util.function.Consumer;

public class OrderStatusPublisher {
    private final List<Consumer<OrderEvent>> observers = new ArrayList<>();

    public void subscribe(Consumer<OrderEvent> observer) {
        observers.add(observer);
    }

    public void statusChanged(String orderId, String status,
                              BigDecimal amount) {
        OrderEvent event = new OrderEvent(orderId, status, amount);
        for (Consumer<OrderEvent> o : observers) {
            try {
                o.accept(event);
            } catch (Exception e) {
                System.err.println("Observer failed: " + e.getMessage());
            }
        }
    }
}

public record OrderEvent(String orderId, String status, BigDecimal amount) {}
```

```java
orderPublisher.subscribe(e -> System.out.println(
    "Email to Priya: Order " + e.orderId() + " is now " + e.status()));
orderPublisher.subscribe(e -> System.out.println(
    "Loyalty: Award " + e.amount().multiply(new BigDecimal("0.01"))
    + " points for order " + e.orderId()));

orderPublisher.statusChanged("ORD-1001", "SHIPPED", new BigDecimal("15000"));
```

```text
Email to Priya: Order ORD-1001 is now SHIPPED
Loyalty: Award 150.00 points for order ORD-1001
```

### How do you handle observer failures?

One slow or failing observer must not block the others. Catch exceptions per observer. For heavy work, dispatch asynchronously.

```java
for (Consumer<OrderEvent> o : observers) {
    try {
        o.accept(event);
    } catch (Exception e) {
        log.warning("Observer failed: " + e.getMessage());
        // Continue notifying remaining observers
    }
}
```

### How does Observer scale to distributed systems?

At scale, the Observer idea becomes publish-subscribe over a message broker like Kafka. The subject publishes an event to a topic, and many consumer services react independently.

```java
// Local Observer
stockPublisher.subscribe(alert -> smsService.send(alert));

// Distributed Observer (Kafka)
kafkaTemplate.send("stock-prices", priceEvent);
// Consumer services subscribe to the topic independently
```

### What are common mistakes?

The first mistake is not unsubscribing observers, causing the lapsed-listener memory leak.

```java
// Wrong: subscribe without unsubscribe plan
publisher.subscribe(observer); // who removes this?

// Correct: track subscription for cleanup
var sub = publisher.subscribe(observer);
// Later: sub.close() or unsubscribe in lifecycle callback
```

The second mistake is modifying the observer list during notification, causing `ConcurrentModificationException`.

### When should you use the Observer pattern?

Use it when a state change in one object should trigger reactions in many independent objects, and the set of reactions changes over time. Do not use it when there is exactly one reaction that never changes.

```java
// Good: multiple independent reactions to a transfer
publisher.subscribe(auditListener);
publisher.subscribe(smsListener);
publisher.subscribe(dashboardListener);

// Over-engineering: only one reaction, never changes
publisher.subscribe(auditListener); // just call auditService directly
```

### What should a senior engineer know?

The Observer pattern is the conceptual foundation of event-driven architecture. Getting failure isolation and unsubscription right is what makes it production-grade. At National Bank of India, Pankaj uses Observer for stock price thresholds and order status changes, and at scale it becomes publish-subscribe over Kafka where consumer services react independently to events on a topic.
